>
>After thinking about it for a while, I've realized that overlaying
>Hack is an interesting problem with some subtleties that require some
>careful thought.
>
>The philosphy behind LRU swapping is that you should swap out the
>stuff that hasn't been used in a while because you are likely to keep
>doing what you've been doing (i.e., executing the code you recently
>executed).

[Scott goes on to explain his (well-founded but IMHO sub-relevant,
_vide infra_) opinion that players' moves are not sufficiently
homogeneous in time to warrant lru replacement]

Hm. I have realised recently that one of the (less constructive)
reasons this discussion continues is that there are a couple of things
neither Norm nor I has thought to mention (as I recall)....

The first is that the PCNetHack overlay system works largely *without*
source modification (lest we make the code even more unmaintainable);
we want a system that will continue to function 'even' when maintained
by a non-overlay-guru (as the majority of folks are). In consequence,
the things we are swapping in and out are presently just the standard
object modules: fairly random chunks of code, and often *not* directly
relatable to the commands you issue.

The second is that most of what happens on a non-sterile level has
*nothing* to do with your commands: it has to do with the actions of
monsters. And those are pretty uniform, for any given 'ecology'.
Furthermore, even if there were a module we *knew* we would need to
process the player's next turn, we were still better off ditching it
rather than incurring (should this happen) two disk accesses PER
SOLDIER on the level, in consequence of a thrash....

The third is that running on a PC with 550K of RAM available, about
300K of the total is dedicated to holding overlays. There are 63
overlays, totalling 800K-300K=500K (there's a 'hole' in the root
overlay which the linker intends for swapping; which is why the
figures don't add properly); thus the average overlay is about 10K
long, and there are around 30 of them resident at any one time...
which under favourable conditions should be a reasonable working set.

The fourth is that the overlay manager is invoked FIVE TO TEN THOUSAND
TIMES per turn. Maybe never, maybe twice, maybe five times, the disk
light will go on. Schemes other than lru will likely not do nearly
this well, unless they rely on the kind of intimate knowledge of the
behaviour of the code that will be invalidated from patch to patch....

Finally, the design objective of the overlay manager is that the mean
time between misses be made significantly *greater* than one turn.
Analytically, it appears that the programme is not so large that this
cannot be acheived (in fact we have had versions in-house that did not
access the disk even in heavy combat on the castle level, though they
were not 'playable' for other reasons). And I am still most optimistic
that we can accomplish it (perhaps not in pl7, but within the lifetime
of version 3, certainly). At that point we *can* begin to ask whether
the cycle of player turns is short enough to be satisfied in the space
available. And it seems to me that when performance is most important
is precisely when this happy circumstance is most likely: when
performing a run or a timed occupation. Otherwise you, the player,
wait to analyse feedback anyway....

In sum, I think Scott's analysis is correct but misguided: while
players' _actions_ are quite random,

(1) the game simulation clock often advances several times during one
interaction round, with the character (perhaps not the player)
repeating essentially the same action each tick;

(2) the grain of the overlays does not map well onto that of user
commands;

(3) one disk access does not take long in user time (even on my clunky
XT it is only the vaguest distraction); but it is a huge penalty in
CPU terms; and

(4) one turn is a LONG time by the overlay manager's standards, and
the reference patterns it is most relevant to analyse are those WITHIN
a turn, not those without it; and these change slowly with time.

Finally, of course, there is the software engineering question: how
would we make a system that had more detailed knowledge of the
statistical behaviour of the programme maintainable?

(We're working on an answer to that.)
